WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.980
Expansion may place entire galaxies permanently beyond any telescope's reach. This is Fascinating Frontiers Episode 230. From that horizon, a Mars rover's distance record, a star forming cauldron in the large Magellanic Cloud, and a new reading of how the Milky Way's disks formed. Picture a walkway that keeps speeding up under your feet. People walking away eventually outrun any message you could send. A horizon fixed by the expansion rate already walls off whole regions,

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.640
and that horizon grows as time passes.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.600
And Dramada's starlight set out 2.5 million years ago,

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:39.080
so a telescope aimed there records the galaxy as it was

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:40.700
before modern humans appeared.

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.260
Observers there, looking our way,

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.480
would find Earth occupied only by distant ancestors.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.740
They would not see cities or machines.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:50.700
They would see an ancient planet.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:53.520
The same stretching of space stretches the light,

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.780
sliding distant signals out of reach.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:00.560
The cosmic microwave background still lets us map the sky from when the universe was

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.380
380,000 years old.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.240
We cannot see past the current particle horizon.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.300
We read information already sent, not the universe as it stands this second.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.940
Regions beyond that horizon could hold stars, planets, oceans, and minds asking whether

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:17.040
anyone else exists.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:19.740
They would not be silent because they vanished.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.380
Geometry would leave them silent to us.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:27.800
A civilization in Andromeda could be searching now, and a reply from us would still need that

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.520
same crossing before it arrived.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.720
A detection would show who they were, not who they are.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.260
They might have advanced, ended, or never been there by the time the light finished the

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:37.480
trip.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.340
Some regions expansion may cut off for good.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:45.200
Some we might one day register only through light that left millions or billions of years

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:45.560
ago.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.360
Some sit so far that a single exchange would make a conversation almost impossible.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:55.640
Star's may already have died whose light has not reached Earth, and planets may have formed

00:01:55.640 --> 00:02:00.360
or broken while their glow is still on the way. Building a larger telescope does not

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:06.260
erase those barriers of distance and time. Earth is one planet around one star in one galaxy

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:12.080
among an almost incomprehensible number, and our civilization occupies a tiny slice of cosmic

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.880
time. There is no definitive evidence of intelligent life elsewhere, and not every star or planet

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:23.260
has been searched. If life is common, finding one another may still be rarer than being alive.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:29.587
Things can be real and still permanently unknowable from here. Perseverance has set a new record

00:02:29.587 --> 00:02:35.667
for distance driven on another celestial body. The rover reached 45.22 kilometers on

00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:41.027
Seoul 2006, according to the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, or NASA.

00:02:41.707 --> 00:02:47.267
The team raised the allowable tilt to 24 degrees and eased belly pan clearance after thousands of

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:53.167
simulations. On board global localization now runs on a commercial processor, first

00:02:53.167 --> 00:02:58.927
flown on ingenuity, that matches ground images to orbital maps so the rover can fix its position

00:02:58.927 --> 00:03:03.547
without waiting for Earth. Breaking software was rewritten to dodge rock hazards,

00:03:04.207 --> 00:03:09.087
steer motor current limits were nearly doubled, and dynamic slip tables now model expected

00:03:09.087 --> 00:03:14.907
slip on each drive. Those changes led it cover longer stretches with less ground team intervention,

00:03:14.907 --> 00:03:17.347
while crossing bolder fields and slopes.

00:03:17.967 --> 00:03:21.287
An engineering twin on Earth received a Guinness World Records plaque,

00:03:21.727 --> 00:03:26.907
and later drives will test generative AI to propose efficient paths along strategic routes.

00:03:27.916 --> 00:03:33.676
The James Webb Space Telescope captured a turbulent star-forming region named N159

00:03:33.676 --> 00:03:38.436
in the Large Magellanic Cloud, 161,000 light-years away.

00:03:38.996 --> 00:03:43.236
The frame spans about 230 light-years and shows dense gas clumps

00:03:43.236 --> 00:03:48.076
where giant molecular clouds are falling in under gravity to make new stars, according

00:03:48.076 --> 00:03:50.616
to the European Space Agency, or ESA.

00:03:51.316 --> 00:03:56.136
Massive hot stars ionize nearby hydrogen and light up brilliant emission nebulae.

00:03:56.796 --> 00:04:01.656
Webb's near infrared camera shows stellar winds cutting ridges and cavities, with light

00:04:01.656 --> 00:04:06.436
orange polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons and pale blue H2 gas in the frame.

00:04:07.096 --> 00:04:11.436
Cool molecular hydrogen appears as dark red filaments that shield the youngest stars

00:04:11.436 --> 00:04:12.216
from radiation.

00:04:12.216 --> 00:04:17.496
The region is less than 3 million years old, holds thousands of stars still in early

00:04:17.496 --> 00:04:22.456
formation, and its lower metalicity compared with the Milky Way matches conditions expected

00:04:22.456 --> 00:04:23.656
in the early universe.

00:04:24.156 --> 00:04:28.296
Stars less than a tenth the mass of the sun were detected in this cloud for the first

00:04:28.296 --> 00:04:33.656
time, with more young stellar objects packed on the western side, a sign that side is at

00:04:33.656 --> 00:04:39.036
an earlier stage of collapse, and the Papillon Nebula sits as a compact blob of ionized

00:04:39.036 --> 00:04:40.756
hydrogen on the left of the image.

00:04:40.756 --> 00:04:47.014
Gaia's astrometry has now ordered the sequence in which the Milky Way's thin and thick discs formed.

00:04:47.434 --> 00:04:53.414
Precise ages for individual stars trace how those disc components assembled, according to universe today.

00:04:54.439 --> 00:04:58.859
The spacecraft's positional accuracy is what made it possible to put the events in order.

00:04:59.499 --> 00:05:04.459
An international team that included University of Michigan researchers found that supernova winds

00:05:04.459 --> 00:05:08.839
played a smaller role in re-ionizing the early universe than earlier work had assumed.

00:05:08.839 --> 00:05:14.639
Hubble observations showed that other processes contributed more to that transition, according

00:05:14.639 --> 00:05:15.739
to Fizz.org.

00:05:16.259 --> 00:05:21.359
The result challenges models that had put supernova-driven winds at the center of reionization.

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:27.150
NASA science is introducing competition model 2.0 to shorten mission selection.

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:32.490
Proposals will lead with the science case, the science implementation plan, and a proof

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:36.870
of concept mission design, stripping out roughly half the material once required.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:41.710
The aim is to cut time from announcement of opportunity to step two selection by up

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:46.270
to 45% from 35 months to about 19.5 months.

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:49.630
Flagship missions keep their existing reformed process.

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:55.770
Astro probes, new frontiers, and discovery will use the accelerated two-step path, with

00:05:55.770 --> 00:06:01.430
implementation studies at Goddard or the Jet Propulsion Laboratory or JPL, while Midex

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:05.790
and Earth Explorer missions will judge implementing organizations on past performance

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:11.470
before selection. Principle investigators without major university or commercial backing are to

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:17.867
receive fair consideration under the new model. Test teams fired a developmental RS-25 engine

00:06:17.867 --> 00:06:24.247
for almost 8.5 minutes at Stennis Space Center yesterday. The engine reached the 111% power

00:06:24.247 --> 00:06:30.747
level required for new production engines on the Space Launch System, or SLS. The hot fire validated

00:06:30.747 --> 00:06:35.707
a new thermal protection system for the nozzle and acceptance tested flight components for future

00:06:35.707 --> 00:06:36.587
